## INSTANCE 2026-10-22 13:14:51 → 13:25:52 PDT — the forecast's 4B rung arrived 20 minutes early, from the other consumer, one counter increment past the value the arm named

Sample bracket: the persisted sample at **13:14:51** (`planner_at=planner kev_window=open`, `no_change`, agent suppressed) and the monitor sample at **13:25:52** (`planner_at=planner kev_window=closed`) ⇒ the transition lies inside a 11 m 01 s bracket; `planner_at` is IDENTICAL on both sides, so **no global→planner flip, no `@ocho` DM** (his rows re-read at write: `profiles/ocho/state.db` id **9249** user / **9267** assistant, `max(id)` **16551** at `2026-10-20 12:56:41` — nothing newer on the scoped-planner thread).

### The close edge, dated on three artifacts inside one second-class

`scopes/planner/record.json` birth **13:20:06.357** (492 B) owner `llama-swap-ocho-planner-4b` class `batch` need **3800** + headroom 256 = 4056, `mode=run`, `pid 151226`, `acquired_at 1792700406` = 13:20:06, `expires_at 1792786806` = **2026-10-23 13:20:06**, `ttl 86400`; wrapper **151226 ALIVE** (S, started 13:20:06) with child **151312 ALIVE** (Sl, 3482 MiB, port 5806); `/running` one entry `ocho-planner-4b state=ready`, `/health` `{"status":"ok"}`; capture `/tmp/gpu-lease.6Jpcyq` mtime **13:20:11.082** carrying `load_model: loading model '…/Qwen3.5-4B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf'` + `model loaded` + `listening on 127.0.0.1:5806` and **one served task** (prompt eval 3654 tok @ 2897.97 tok/s, eval 125 tok @ 96.38 tok/s); `plans/latest_plan.json` **13:20:11.180** `model_slot ocho-planner-4b`, and `ocho_predictions.jsonl` row `2026-10-22T20:20:11.180580+00:00`. So the close is a holder that **loaded and did the lane's work**, not a refusal and not a round's exit status (`2026-10-22-a-close-belongs-to-the-holder-that-loaded-not-the-round-that-failed`).

### What the forecast got right and what it got wrong

The 12:53 instance forecast `13:40 = 59266 = pos 6 = the first 4B pick ⇒ close ≈13:40:3x, width ≈3600 s`. Measured: `model_cycle_counter.txt` = **59267**, mtime **13:20:06.265680913** — the same second as the lease birth — and `59267 mod 10 = 7`, while `59266 mod 10 = 6`, both of which the pick map reads as **4B**. The **rung identity was right** (a 4B pick at pos 6/7 is what closed the card) and **the round it was assigned to was wrong**: the ledger read 59262 at 12:50 and the counter stood at 59267 at 13:20:06, i.e. **+5 across three rounds** (13:00, 13:10, 13:20 — the 13:10 round's journal is `status=1/FAILURE` 5.892 s and the 13:20 round `Finished` 10.708 s), so the counter does **not** advance one per round: the 13:00 tick carries the hourly `ocho-shadow-polymarket` runner (`13:00:23 → 13:01:20`, `polymarket_paper_trader.py --llm` → `call_ocho_planner.py`) beside the planner round, which is the same two-draws-one-tick structure the 07:01 tick identified. A rung→round mapping built at one draw per round therefore **hands the predicted rung to an earlier round than the one it names**, and pos 6 arrived at 13:20, twenty minutes before the forecast's 13:40.

The stated falsifier was `free ≥ 4289 at a sample after 13:41 with a planning model resident, or a closed reading before 13:40 with the counter below 59266`. **Neither arm fired and neither could have**: the counter was already 59267 at 13:20:06, so "closed before 13:40 **with the counter below 59266**" excludes the very case that occurred — an early close at or past the forecast value. The forecast was therefore **pre-empted, not scored**: an arm that pins an exact counter value in a shared, twice-written counter cannot score an earlier arrival. Score such a forecast **by rung identity and by "any counter"**, and date the draw off the counter's **mtime**, not off the round the counter was predicted to belong to.

The close also **held**: the 13:30 round (`13:30:23 → 13:30:31 Finished`, counter **59268** at `13:30:29.257`) wrote plan `2026-10-22T20:30:31.661387+00:00` with **no new `/tmp/gpu-lease.*`** and only the existing capture `/tmp/gpu-lease.6Jpcyq` growing 1637 → 2454 B, i.e. the same wrapper and the same 3482 MiB llama-server served the next round — this is what a `ttl 86400` liveness-first holder looks like from outside: one load, many rounds, the card shut for the whole term rather than for one rotation step.

### Live at write, three timed reads 13:27:18.537 / 13:27:20.572 / 13:27:22.606

**8192 total / 5239 used / FREE 2652 / util 1 % / 38 C ⇒ CLOSED, 1637 MiB under the 4289 Kev-4B gate**; compute apps 151312 = 3482 + 2360 Bonsai = 1470 + 72233 vision = 134 ⇒ 5086 of 5239; the unleased aux floor is **1604 MiB** (both at `--n-gpu-layers 0`), and used+free = 7892 = the ~300 MiB driver reservation, so 6268 remains the empty-card ceiling. The holder is `mode=run` with `ttl 86400` under the standing **liveness-first** policy (a live `mode=run` pid is never reclaimable at any age), so the card stays shut not for a rotation rung but **until llama-swap's next group switch or the holder's exit** — the reason this window did not reopen behind the 13:20 load is a lease term, not a resident model.

